From 382c8b826d14d216f13d0a3a32a8c91198223ced Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bakhodirovxz <bohodirovasadbek1@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 02:25:27 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 2/8] model: formulas can share one tree (shifted copies)

Every formula cell held its own text and syntax tree (about 680 bytes for a typical formula), also
when a column is one formula filled down or an Excel shared formula: on a 10 MB workbook with
432,120 formulas that is half of the peak memory.

A `Formula` is now either its own text and tree, as before, or a shifted copy: another formula's
tree (shared through `Arc`) plus the row and column offset that copy/fill would move it by. A
shifted copy builds its text and tree on demand. Its meaning does not depend on where its cell is
stored (the offset is from the shared tree, not from the cell), so code that moves cells (sort,
insert, cut) keeps working unchanged, and any rewrite simply stores a formula of its own.

- `text` and `expr` become private: `text()` (a `Cow`), `is_parsed()`, and the existing `expr()`
  / `expr_arc()`; the call sites change mechanically.
- `Formula::shifted`, `Formula::copied` (what copy and fill do, sharing the tree when it can) and
  `Formula::template` (the shared tree and offset).
- `FormulaRuns` finds runs of formulas that are the same in R1C1 terms as cells are visited in
  row-major order, for readers and for rewrites.
- `PartialEq` compares by meaning, so a shifted copy equals the same formula held on its own;
  serde keeps the `{text, array}` form.
